ment that says that what's going on  over here in this thinker in this book  or passage or system or tradition or  whatever it is is the same as what's  going on over here and sometimes it's  phrased in a rather you know more  tentative way I kind of think that these  are what's you know going on here and  these are very similar to each other or  couldn't it be the case you know uh I  would like to think that XYZ but it  really comes down to the same thing  thing assuming and asserting  similarities connections that aren't as  strong as we'd think and this can extend  even to making analogies saying if this  is the case over here then we should  also expect this to be the case over  here now I don't want to Simply throw  cold water as they say onto this it's oh  that's air that's awful you don't have  any good grounds for what you're saying  no I don't want to do that  because I think that there's something  important about this I think that this  is  you know a reflection of the way that  the human mind does work as it's  developing and the human mind doesn't  just develop by itself it develops in  relation to other people right some of  whom might be are interlocutors online  or in face-to-face Encounters in the  same time some of whom might be from  books that we're reading you know their  takes their viewpoints right the  important philosophers think about the  fact that we can in fact read Plato and  get his own takes or at least the tanks  that he puts in the mouth of his  characters  on a whole bunch of other thinkers as  well sometimes we look at them and we're  like yeah that's pretty good sometimes  we're like oh he's being unfair to this  person so I I don't want to discourage  anybody from this Behavior I just would  like to say  maybe you want to think about what  you're doing and try to do it in a more  reflective a more prudent way which will  help you out and I think it's just as  important so here's one of the takeaways  from this  it's very exciting to to note  connections to note similarities  it's just as important though perhaps  not quite as exciting to pay attention  to disparities to pay attention through  differences to make distinctions to be  able to compare and contrast to say yes  these things are kind of the same but  they're also not the same they're also  unlike they're different they're perhaps  even opposed to each other in certain  ways I want to say that you you're not  going to be as successful with just  having the one without also bringing in  the other and this goes back to you know  ancient philosophy I think really and  basically every tradition there's a  tendency to classify things together to  on the basis of similarities and then we  have to make differences we have to be  able to say where they're unlike to each  other and how they're different and why  the differences matter so  how does this happen well I think that  one of the most common things one of the  most common ways in which this happens a  dynamic is that people are misled by  language and so it can happen in you  know a very let's call it rudimentary  naive way when somebody reads important  philosophers in a particular language it  will take English which is you know my  native language as an example  so let's say you're reading you know a  translation of Renee Descartes and uh  you know it's the meditations and he's  talking about ideas and thoughts and you  know things like that right and then  you're reading somebody else  say Nietzsche coming from German into  English as well and he's using the word  idea or schopenhauer is using the word  idea and you know you see that word idea  and you're like oh wow we've all got  ideas or we could also take an English  writer like David Hume right uh the word  idea isn't necessarily doing the same  work it's not necessarily referring to  the same thing in all of these different  texts and thinkers and it might not even  be doing exactly the same thing  for the same thinker w